SQL> -- ROAD role administration (spec-patch-06 section 5, spec-patch-07 section 7).
SQL> --
SQL> -- Byte-identical to road-cal's api/modules/admin/module.create.sql apart from the module name and
SQL> -- the absence of POST /admin/purge/, which is a calendar operation. The file is NOT on the parity
SQL> -- list -- ORDS modules diverge deliberately, because they name an application -- so that identity
SQL> -- is a convention to maintain by hand, not one the check will enforce.
SQL> --
SQL> -- The module was named road_cal_api.admin here until 2026-08-19: an unrenamed copy-paste from the
SQL> -- patch 06 backport, while every other road-kit module is hello_api.*. ORDS module names are
SQL> -- schema-scoped so it worked, but road-kit's admin API announced itself as road-cal's.
SQL> --
SQL> -- Every handler here follows the same two-line preamble: establish session context, or 401. ORDS
SQL> -- exposes no schema-callable pre-dispatch hook, so this is per handler by necessity
SQL> -- (spec-patch-06 section 6.3) and bin/check-handler-coverage.sh is what stops one being forgotten.
SQL> --
SQL> -- The handlers are thin. They bind path parameters and the body into one JSON payload and call
SQL> -- road_admin_api, which enforces the permission requirement itself -- so the authorisation travels
SQL> -- with the operation rather than living only in the handler.
SQL> 
SQL> -- Both names: hello_api.admin is current, road_cal_api.admin is what this module was called until
SQL> -- 2026-08-19. Without the second delete, a schema deployed before the rename keeps serving the old
SQL> -- module alongside the new one, on the same base path.
